---
import SplitForHackerNewsShell from '../../../../components/SplitForHackerNewsShell.astro';
import { splitForHackerNews } from '../../../../data/split-for-hacker-news';

const description =
  'Split for Hacker News privacy summary: no telemetry or account, sanitized public URL lookups through Algolia, local settings, and side panel framing behavior.';
---

<SplitForHackerNewsShell
  title="Split for Hacker News Privacy Policy"
  description={description}
  current="privacy"
>
  <article class="pt-16 sm:pt-24">
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">
        Privacy policy
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-5xl leading-[1.06] font-medium text-balance [font-variation-settings:'WONK'_1,'SOFT'_40] sm:text-6xl">
        Split for Hacker News Privacy
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-muted">
        Last updated: <time datetime="2026-09-20">September 20, 2026</time>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-secondary sm:text-xl">
        Split for Hacker News is free, open source (MIT), and unofficial. It has no analytics, telemetry, extension account, advertising, or developer-operated backend. Lookups send eligible public URLs to Algolia; opened discussions connect directly to Hacker News. Article text is never read.
      </p>
    </header>

    <aside class="mt-12 rounded-2xl border border-accent/25 bg-accent-soft p-6 sm:p-8" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
      <h2 id="summary-title" class="font-display text-2xl font-medium">Plain-language summary</h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 grid gap-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-secondary sm:grid-cols-2">
        <li class="rounded-lg bg-card/70 px-4 py-3">Article text and other page content are never read</li>
        <li class="rounded-lg bg-card/70 px-4 py-3">No analytics, extension account, advertising, or developer-operated backend</li>
        <li class="rounded-lg bg-card/70 px-4 py-3">Only eligible, sanitized public URLs can be sent to Algolia</li>
        <li class="rounded-lg bg-card/70 px-4 py-3">Settings stay local; lookup data lasts only for the browser session</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>

    <div class="mt-16 max-w-4xl space-y-14 sm:mt-20">
      <section aria-labelledby="lookup-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Manual by default</p>
        <h2 id="lookup-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">What a discussion lookup processes</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          When you open the extension popup, it reads the active page URL and, if present, the URL in that page’s canonical link. Before a search, it removes fragments and recognized tracking parameters and rejects private, local, special-use, credential-bearing, or apparently secret-bearing URLs. Eligible public URL candidates are sent over HTTPS to Algolia’s public Hacker News Search API and checked locally for an exact match.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          The extension does not read the article body, forms, messages, or other page content. Algolia is the only third party that receives lookup candidates from the extension and may process ordinary connection metadata under its own{' '}
          <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href="https://www.algolia.com/policies/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          Filtering rejects recognized secrets and private addresses; it cannot guarantee that every eligible public URL is anonymous.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="entrypoints-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">User control</p>
        <h2 id="entrypoints-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">Optional and explicit entry points</h2>
        <div class="mt-7 divide-y divide-line overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-line">
          <div class="grid gap-2 bg-card p-5 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
            <h3 class="font-medium">Automatic badge</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-secondary">Off by default. When enabled, eligible public tab URLs can be sent to Algolia as tabs navigate so the toolbar can show a comment count. Turning it off stops checks and clears badges and lookup cache entries.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="grid gap-2 p-5 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
            <h3 class="font-medium">Side-panel following</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-secondary">Side-panel following is a separate opt-in, off by default. It sends eligible active-tab URLs to Algolia only while a panel is already open. Check this tab runs once without enabling following. Neither action opens or rearranges tabs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="grid gap-2 p-5 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
            <h3 class="font-medium">Open in Split</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-secondary">The context-menu item receives a link URL only after you select it. The extension opens that link in the clicked tab and applies the same eligibility and sanitization boundary before any lookup.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="grid gap-2 bg-card p-5 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
            <h3 class="font-medium">HN story clicks</h3>
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-secondary">Off by default. When enabled, a content script limited to news.ycombinator.com observes a story-link click and reads that link and item ID. The click handler keeps the link and item ID on the device and performs no Algolia lookup. Opening the article and HN discussion still makes ordinary requests to those sites. This shortcut is not included in Firefox.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="storage-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Storage and retention</p>
        <h2 id="storage-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">What stays in Chrome</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          The automatic-badge, side-panel-follow, and Hacker News story-click settings are stored in <code>chrome.storage.local</code>. Time-bounded lookup results, selected side-panel content, and article-to-discussion tab associations use <code>chrome.storage.session</code>, which Chrome discards when the browser session ends. Found URL lookups may be reused for one hour and not-found lookups for ten minutes; failures and restricted pages are not added to that cache. The extension does not create a persistent browsing history.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="panel-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Real Hacker News, embedded</p>
        <h2 id="panel-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">How the side panel works</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          The side panel embeds the real <code>news.ycombinator.com</code> discussion. Hacker News receives ordinary request metadata and any cookies the browser allows for that embedded request; cookie behavior need not match a normal tab. The extension does not read the framed page, its cookies, or your Hacker News session.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          Hacker News normally blocks framing. While the panel is open, the extension temporarily removes <code>X-Frame-Options</code>, <code>Content-Security-Policy</code>, and <code>Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only</code> headers from Hacker News sub-frame responses. The rule is installed when the first panel connects and removed after the last panel closes, never affects top-level Hacker News pages or another domain, and does not read, block, or redirect requests. Because Chrome cannot scope this rule to one extension frame, the temporary exception applies to any Hacker News sub-frame in that browser profile while a panel is open. Removing the full CSP headers also removes Hacker News’s script restrictions and report-only policy inside those frames.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          Each panel can retain up to three recent HN documents in memory for faster return. Extension code does not read their DOM, cookies, focus, or scroll position. Closing or reloading the panel, a reconnect, eviction, extension update, or browser memory pressure can discard them; scroll restoration is best effort. This is separate from session lookup metadata.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="permissions-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Documented capabilities</p>
        <h2 id="permissions-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">Why the extension asks for permissions</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          Chrome permissions support reading eligible tab and canonical URLs after the relevant action, running the short-lived canonical-link check, storing settings and session state, adding the one context-menu item, opening the side panel, and applying the temporary Hacker News sub-frame rule. The tabs permission also supports automatic URL checks only after opt-in; the scripting permission supports the optional HN story-click handler in Chromium builds. Host access is limited to Algolia’s Hacker News API and Hacker News itself. Firefox uses Sidebar instead of the sidePanel permission and omits the story-click handler. The complete permission-by-permission explanation is maintained in the canonical policy linked below.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="control-title">
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Your choices</p>
        <h2 id="control-title" class="font-display text-3xl font-medium">Control and contact</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-secondary">
          Keep all available automatic settings off to use manual lookups only. Uninstalling the extension removes its local storage. Privacy questions can be sent to{' '}
          <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={`mailto:${splitForHackerNews.contactEmail}`}>{splitForHackerNews.contactEmail}</a>{' '}
          or reported through{' '}
          <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={splitForHackerNews.issuesUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Issues</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-16 border-t border-line pt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
      This page is a readable summary. The canonical, version-controlled policy is{' '}
      <a class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4" href={splitForHackerNews.privacySourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">PRIVACY.md on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</SplitForHackerNewsShell>
